# Git Commit Message Format Guidelines

<introduction>
This reference documents the conventions for creating Git commit messages following the Conventional Commits standard. It provides a structured approach to creating consistent, readable, and machine-parsable commit history that facilitates automated versioning and changelog generation.
</introduction>

<context>
- Creating commit messages for new changes
- Reviewing commit message format in pull requests
- Automating version control and changelog generation
- Maintaining consistent project history
</context>

<format_specification>
The standard commit message format follows this pattern:

`<type>(<scope>): <description>`

Where:
- `type`: Identifies the kind of change being made
- `scope`: Optional area of the codebase affected (in parentheses)
- `description`: A concise summary of the changes
</format_specification>

<commit_types>
- `feat`: A new feature implementation
- `fix`: A bug fix or correction
- `docs`: Documentation-only changes
- `style`: Changes to code style without affecting logic
- `refactor`: Code changes that neither fix bugs nor add features
- `perf`: Code changes that improve performance
- `test`: Adding or correcting tests
- `chore`: Changes to build process or auxiliary tools
</commit_types>

<scope_guidelines>
The scope should be derived from the file path or affected component:
- Use directory structure with hyphens replacing slashes
- Keep scopes consistent across similar changes
- Use broader scopes for changes affecting multiple components
- Omit scope when changes affect many areas of the codebase
</scope_guidelines>

<description_standards>
A well-formed commit description should be:
- Written in imperative mood ("add feature" not "added feature")
- Clear and concise (ideally under 50 characters)
- Started with a lowercase letter
- Free of trailing punctuation
- Focused on what the change does, not why or how
</description_standards>

<body_guidelines>
For commits with multiple changes or needing additional context:
- Separate header from body with a blank line
- Format as a bulleted list for multiple related changes
- Start each bullet with an imperative verb
- Focus each bullet on a single logical change
- Provide enough detail to understand the change
</body_guidelines>

<breaking_changes>
- Indicate breaking changes with an exclamation mark after the type/scope: `feat(api)!: change authentication API`
- Include BREAKING CHANGE: at the beginning of a body paragraph for additional details
- Explain the nature of the breaking change and migration path if applicable
- Consider separating breaking changes into their own commits when possible
- Document API changes thoroughly in the commit body
</breaking_changes>

<warning>
Always analyze the actual working diff before creating a commit message. Commit messages that don't accurately reflect the changes made create confusion during future code archaeology and can lead to misunderstandings about when specific changes were introduced.
</warning>

<examples>
<example>
// Feature addition with simple description
// Working diff shows new authentication function in src/auth/login.ts

feat(mitsubishi-citizen): add tool offset gathering
</example>

<example type="alternative">
// Bug fix with body providing additional context
// Working diff shows fixes in date handling across multiple files

fix(fanuc-hfcollector): correct data gathering timestamp anomaly in hfcollector

- Fix out of order timestamps in the csv writer
- Update thread sync handling for between csv writes and nats publish
- Add validation to prevent invalid date inputs
</example>

<example type="alternative">
// Breaking change with explanatory body
// Working diff shows major API changes

feat(fanuc-api)!: updated fanuc library with a mythical major revision

BREAKING CHANGE: User management API now requires authentication token
for all operations. The previous session-based authentication is removed.

- Add token validation middleware
- Remove session management code
- Update documentation with migration guide
</example>
</examples>

<critical_points>
- ALWAYS analyze the ACTUAL DIFF before writing commit messages
- USE the correct type to indicate the primary purpose of the change
- INCLUDE a scope when changes affect a specific component
- WRITE descriptions in imperative mood for consistency
- ADD detailed bullet points in the body for complex changes
- INDICATE breaking changes with ! after the type/scope
- SEPARATE related changes into multiple commits when appropriate
- MAINTAIN consistency in scope naming across the project
- REVIEW commit messages in pull requests for compliance with this format
- NEVER include sensitive information in commit messages
</critical_points>